{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-2551d682-6e1b-4676-8b0b-d24f71d53800","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9df299725fffdd80","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9006-s197mafq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9006-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9006: Feature Store as a supported RHOAI component\n\nRHAISTRAT-9006 brings Feast in as a supported, installable RHOAI component. Feast\nis a **new component**: it has a reserved Jira component (Feature Store) but no\narchitecture context file, no productized repository, no build pipeline, and no\noperator integration. The decomposition therefore combines a new-component\nonboarding chain (repo + license + Konflux) with the integration work across the\nPlatform Operator, Notebooks (workbench images), and Dashboard, plus a\ndocs-authoring epic.\n\nThe strategy carries one genuine unknown — which Feast deployment topology to\nproductize first (operator-based vs. standalone server per project). The answer\nchanges what the deployment productization, the Platform Operator integration,\nand the Dashboard endpoint surface do, so it is resolved by an Investigation\nepic with two bounded conditional branches.\n\n## Epic List (main plan)\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-E001 | Decide the Feast deployment topology to productize first | Investigation | RHOAI Feature Store | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-E002 | Create the midstream Feast repository under opendatahub-io | Implementation (repo-onboarding) | RHOAI Feature Store | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-E003 | Validate licenses for Feast and its transitive dependencies | Implementation (license-validation) | RHOAI Feature Store | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-E004 | Onboard the Feast repository to Konflux | Implementation (konflux-onboarding) | RHOAI Feature Store | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-E005 | Enable Feature Store as a managed component in the Platform Operator | Implementation | RHOAI Platform Operator | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-E006 | Preinstall the Feast SDK in a supported workbench image | Implementation | RHOAI Notebooks | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-E007 | Surface Feature Store enablement and endpoints in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-E008 | Document Feature Store enablement, getting started, and supported limits | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n\n### Conditional branch epics (not standalone Jira issues)\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Gate |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-BRANCH-A-E009 | Productize the operator-based Feast deployment | Implementation | RHOAI Feature Store | P0 | remove |\n| RHAISTRAT-9006-BRANCH-B-E009 | Productize the standalone per-project Feast server deployment | Implementation | RHOAI Feature Store | P0 | remove |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\nMain plan (arrows point from dependency to dependent):\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[E001 topology Investigation] --> E005[E005 Platform Operator enablement]\n    E001 --> E007[E007 Dashboard integration]\n    E002[E002 repo-onboarding] --> E004[E004 konflux-onboarding]\n    E003[E003 license-validation] --> E005\n    E003 --> E006[E006 workbench SDK image]\n    E002 --> E008[E008 docs]\n    E003 --> E008\n    E004 --> E008\n    E005 --> E008\n    E006 --> E008\n    E007 --> E008\n```\n\nConditional branches (attached to the Investigation as a plan document; exactly\none branch's epic is executed after the Investigation resolves):\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[E001 topology Investigation] --> BRANCHA[BRANCH-A-E009 operator-based deployment]\n    E001 --> BRANCHB[BRANCH-B-E009 standalone per-project server]\n    E002[E002 repo-onboarding] --> BRANCHA\n    E002 --> BRANCHB\n    E003[E03 license-validation] --> BRANCHA\n    E003 --> BRANCHB\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 | The topology decision rewrites the operator integration approach (enable the Feast operator vs. instantiate per-project servers). True gate, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E007 | Rule 3 | The CR/endpoint surface the Dashboard reads differs per topology. True gate, `rewrite`. |\n| E002 ∥ E003 (parallel roots) | Rule 7 | repo-onboarding and license-validation have independent inputs; both start immediately. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 5 | The Konflux pipeline needs the repository to exist; always serial. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 9 | If licenses fail, the dependency set and approach of the general implementation change; serial. |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 9 | The Feast SDK baked into a workbench image must be license-clear (with its transitive deps) first. |\n| E001 → BRANCH-A/B-E009 | Rule 3 | The Investigation selects which branch epic exists; `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` on both. |\n| E002 → BRANCH-A/B-E009 | Rule 6 | Deployment code is written in the onboarded midstream repo; repo must exist first. Does not block other repos (E005/E006/E007). |\n| E003 → BRANCH-A/B-E009 | Rules 9, 20 | General implementation of the new component is serial after license-validation. |\n| E004 ∥ BRANCH-A/B-E009 (no edge) | Rule 10 | Konflux onboarding and the deployment implementation run in parallel; coupling is enforced by a \"build pipeline green\" AC on the implementation, never by a DAG edge. Likewise no E004 → E005/E006/E007 edges. |\n| BRANCH deployment images → E005 (no edge) | Rule 13 (exception) | The image digests the operator references are configuration references — AC-gated (\"pinned productized image digests available\"), not a build-against dependency. |\n| E005 ∥ E007 (no edge) | Rule 14 | Different repos (Platform Operator vs. Dashboard), no shared build-against artifact at plan time; both consume the topology decision. |\n| E006 ∥ E005/E007 (no edge) | Rule 14 | Different repo (workbench images); SDK preinstall is independent of deployment topology. |\n| E002…E007 → E008 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring is blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic; docs always last. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epics |\n|-----|----------|------------------------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | Admins enable supported Feast via Platform Operator with productized images | E002, E003, E004, E005, BRANCH-A-E009, BRANCH-B-E009 |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | Feast SDK preinstalled in at least one supported workbench image | E006 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | Dashboard shows Feature Store as enabled and links to per-project endpoints | E007 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | Docs cover enablement, getting-started, supported limits | E008 |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-1 is collectively\ndelivered by the onboarding chain, the Platform Operator integration, and the\ntopology-conditional deployment productization.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\n- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n- **Notebooks (non-obvious mapping):** HLR-2 requires the Feast SDK in a\n  supported workbench image, but the strategy's Affected Components list does\n  not name Notebooks. Workbench images are owned by the Notebooks component, so\n  the workbench SDK epic is assigned `component=Notebooks`.\n- **Feature Store** is a canonical component name but has **no architecture\n  context file** → treated as a new component; full onboarding chain generated\n  (Rule 20).\n- One epic spanning several strategy components: E005 (Platform Operator) is the\n  single most-relevant canonical name for the operator enablement work; the\n  Feast deployment itself lives in the Feature Store branch epics.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections:** the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n   Non-Functional Requirements. Team assignments were inferred (Dashboard's\n   team taken from its architecture context file; others named after their\n   components) and epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n2. **Priority inversion risk (docs):** HLR-4 is P2, but per the docs-authoring\n   priority exception the docs epic derives P1 from the strategy's `priority:\n   Major` frontmatter. Flagged for human review.\n3. **Scope trap watch:** the strategy rules out managed offline-store\n   infrastructure (customers bring their own storage). The docs epic must state\n   this as a supported limit, and no epic should introduce offline-store\n   provisioning — the ACs reflect that.\n4. **Out-of-scope guard:** the Dashboard epic is link-out only; the strategy\n   explicitly defers a feature-authoring UI. The Dashboard epic's ACs enforce\n   this boundary.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. The strategy has no external dependency with uncertain timing;\nthe only unknown (deployment topology) is resolved internally by the\nInvestigation epic and its bounded branches.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nThe topology Investigation (E001) has exactly two bounded outcomes, each of\nwhich changes which deployment epic exists:\n\n- **Branch A — operator-based deployment** (`RHAISTRAT-9006-BRANCH-A-E009`):\n  productize the operator-based topology. `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`\n  — if the operator-based topology is not selected, this epic is removed and\n  branch B executes instead.\n- **Branch B — standalone server per project**\n  (`RHAISTRAT-9006-BRANCH-B-E009`): productize the standalone per-project\n  server topology. `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` — if the standalone\n  topology is not selected, branch A executes instead.\n\nBoth branch epics depend on the Investigation (their `gated_by`), the midstream\nrepository, and license validation; both run in parallel with Konflux\nonboarding (Rule 10, AC-gated). Downstream epics that depend on the\nInvestigation outcome (E005, E007)